Week 4: Predictive Modelling
Overview
This task was focused on creating a machine learning framework to predict future performance in an agribusiness scenario. Using the Daily Commodity Prices India dataset, I built a regression framework to predict the modal price of commodities and compared various machine learning models.

Objectives
To create:
Numerical and categorical features.
Use StandardScaler for numerical features. Use OneHotEncoder for categorical features.
Training and comparing various regression models.
Evaluate based on MAE, RMSE and R squared score.
Identify the best performing model.

 Methodology
Data was first cleaned to convert the arrival date into the correct format and remove any missing data for the target price and dates. The numerical features were: commodity code, year, month, day of week, and quarter. Categorical features were: state, district, market, commodity, variety, and grade.

A pipeline was created which contained StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder and imputation techniques. This was split into a training and test set. The data was split sequentially with 80% for training and 20% for testing.

Models compared were:
Linear Regression
Ridge Regression
Lasso Regression
ElasticNet
Decision Tree
Random Forest
Extra Trees
AdaBoost
Gradient Boosting
K-Nearest Neighbours
Support Vector Regression

 Results
The best performing model was Random Forest which had results:

MAE: 216.22
RMSE: 807.07
R Squared: 0.679
Negative predictions: 0

This means the best model out of all the models was Random Forest. However there is still some error in the predictions so further validation will be needed.

